# <%= @name %>

An Edgeway app.

```sh
bin/setup                                     # mise, bundle, <%= PackageManager.name %> and the local D1 migrations, in one go

# or step by step:
mise install                                  # Spinel and the WASI SDK (mise.toml)
bundle install
<%= PackageManager.install %>
bundle exec edgeway build                     # build/app.wasm (or bin/build); --incremental, for development
<%= PackageManager.exec %> wrangler d1 migrations apply <%= @name %>-db --local
<%= PackageManager.exec %> wrangler dev                              # http://localhost:8787/health
bundle exec edgeway routes
```

`bin/deploy` builds with `--release`: one unit, without Spinel's `--incremental` (cached C units, no inlining across units), for deploying.

Without mise, set `SPINEL` to the Spinel compiler and `WASI_SDK` to the WASI SDK.

Tests (`test/`) run with `bin/test [FILE...]` (all of `test/` when no file is given; it runs `bundle exec edgeway test`). Always use it rather than `spinel_test-unit` directly: it rewrites `tmp/test_app.rb` (git-ignored) from `app/` on every run, so new files are picked up. The tests are compiled to wasm like the app and run by node on wrangler's local D1 (in memory, so `.wrangler/state` is not touched); each test gets an emptied database with `db/migrations/*.sql` applied. This needs the tools of `bin/setup` (Spinel, the WASI SDK, Node.js and wrangler).

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs `bin/test` and `bin/build --release` on GitHub Actions for pushes to main and pull requests.

Model tests inherit `Edgeway::UnitTest` (a database, no requests), request tests `Edgeway::IntegrationTest` (a `UnitTest` that also has `get`, `post`, `json`, `assert_response`).
